  "image_name": "2a17f172f45eea1e22543610bbcb45f9.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a healthy brain with a brain affected by Alzheimer’s disease (AD), focusing on how microglia influence oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs) and myelin maintenance. In the healthy brain, microglia release supportive factors that promote OPC self-division, differentiation, and migration. These precursor cells then mature into oligodendrocytes, which wrap neuronal axons with myelin, supporting normal neuron structure and function.\n\nIn the AD brain, this supportive relationship appears disrupted. Microglia are shown in a senescent, dysfunctional state and are associated with amyloid deposits. Instead of beneficial signals, they release inflammatory or harmful factors labeled SASP, and a lysosomal storage deficit is indicated. Red X marks show that OPC self-renewal, differentiation, and migration are impaired. As a result, fewer effective oligodendrocytes are produced, and the myelin sheath around the axon appears fragmented or degraded.\n\nOverall, the image presents AD as a condition in which altered, aging microglia fail to support oligodendrocyte lineage cells and may actively damage the environment, contributing to defective remyelination and neuronal decline.",
